          "source_text_sha256": "2ac89907c822dd3ea71a84f3e2c406df74743e9802b3fa135b08309e9e53a919",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "needs-review",
          "text": "Description: On June 12, 2010, between 2:00 and 2:30 PM, several people gathered around a barbecue noticed in the sky a black, triangular object at a high altitude. The object appeared stationary, then took an ascending trajectory before disappearing behind a cloud. There was a north/northwestern wind that day. The witness was observing in the north/northwestern direction, which is into the wind: therefore, an ascending balloon following the wind would appear to move very little to the observer. The triangular shape of the object suggests a tetrahedral balloon, black because seen in backlighting by the sun at the zenith. This balloon could be a weather balloon launched from Blagnac or Meteo-France, or a toy balloon released from the city center (many end-of-year celebrations were taking place in Toulouse that day). The object's movement, color, and shape are consistent with a hydrogen or helium-filled balloon carried by the wind. This case is classified as \"B\": probable observation of a balloon.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "e11433c5b4381304e5f866df51f013ad7d5f410d7d811b99d9d9946b69aaa55d"
        }
